1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Nc3 a6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. Be3 Bb4 7. Qd2 Nf6 8.
Bg5 $2 Nxe4 9. Nxc6 Qxg5 10. Qxg5 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Nxg5 12. Ne5 d5 13. f3 f6 14.
Ng4 Bd7 15. Bd3 Rc8 16. Kd2 O-O 17. Rab1 b5 18. Rhe1 Nf7 19. f4 e5 20. Nf2 Rc5
21. fxe5 Nxe5 22. Re3 Rfc8 23. Nd1 Nc4+ 24. Bxc4 Rxc4 25. Rd3 Bg4 26. Ne3 Rf4
27. Rf1 Rxf1 28. Nxf1 Be6 29. Ne3 Rd8 30. Rd4 Kf7 31. c4 bxc4 32. Nxc4 Ke7 33.
Na5 Rd6 34. Nb3 g6 35. Nc5 Rc6 36. Nd3 Rc4 37. Rxc4 dxc4 38. Nb2 Kd6 39. Kc3
Kc5 40. Nd1 h5 41. Nf2 Bd5 42. g3 g5 43. Nd1 {Adjudicated a draw by Steinitz.
According to the rules laid down for the occasion, the umpire could only give
the game to either player if able to demonstrate a forced win in six moves.
But 'The Chess Monthly', Vol.1, page 242, felt that the position was a
demonstrable win for Black. White was from Trinity Hall, Cambridge, and Black
from Brasenose College, Oxford. Source: The Chess Player, Vol. 1, 1879-80,
page 241-2. The Field, 20 March 1880 (probably written by Steinitz himself):
"Mr Steinitz declared the game drawn, though Black was a clear P ahead; for by
the rules, a game could only be adjudicated in favour of either party on the
first two boards if a clear win could be demonstrated within the first six
moves. The above position did not warrant such judgment, for if White
judiciously plays his N to f2, where it would alternately move to d1, h1 or h3
accordingly, Black would gain no immediate advantage by pushing his f-pawn on,
nor has Black any prospect of making any successful manoeuvres with his K,
within the specified number of moves."} 1/2-1/2

